vated to keep developing my skills in web development.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/148.0.0.0 Safari/537.36 Edg/148.0.0.0','comment',0,0),(897,913,'Josué Kamo','kamojosue09@gmail.com','https://freedev-group.github.io/josue-clipboard-landing-page/','45.221.7.28','2026-05-23 21:50:46','2026-05-23 21:50:46','This reading helped me understand that testing is not only a technical activity, but also a mindset and a responsibility within a development team. As developers, we often focus on making features work and moving quickly to the next task, especially under time pressure. However, “working” does not always mean “reliable.”\r\n\r\nOne important lesson I learned is the difference between “Done” and “Done Done.” A feature is not truly complete until it has been reviewed, tested from different perspectives, and validated properly. I also found the idea of the “builder mindset” versus the “tester mindset” very interesting. Developers usually think about how to build systems, while testers intentionally try to break them in order to find weaknesses and unexpected behaviors.\r\n\r\nAnother point that stood out to me is how emotionally attached developers can become to their own code. This attachment can make it difficult to notice mistakes objectively. That is why collaboration, code reviews, and independent testing are essential in producing reliable software.\r\n\r\nPersonally, this discussion reminded me that testing should not be treated as an optional or final step. It should be integrated throughout the entire development process. Going forward, I want to improve by testing negative scenarios more intentionally, revisiting old features when adding new ones, and being more open to external feedback and reviews.\r\n\r\nOverall, this was a valuable reflection on quality, teamwork, humility, and responsibility in software development.',0,'0','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KH